# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 has "same_rulers" and "same_rulers → sedition" and "gold_fixed → □same_rulers" and "military_unhappy" and "□(city_happy → some_part_happy)"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 has "same_rulers" and "same_rulers → sedition" and "sedition → hazardous" and "gold_fixed → same_rulers" and "military_unhappy" and "□(city_happy → some_part_happy)"; they differ when only "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "keeping the same persons always in office becomes the cause of sedition", "the golden particle God mixed into the soul does not pass from one to another but always stays with the same", "Socrates deprives the military of happiness", "necessarily, (if the whole city is happy, then all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy)", "necessarily, Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office" and "necessarily, (if the whole city is happy, then all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy)" are true, which the first allows; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3 has "same_rulers" and "same_rulers → sedition" and "sedition → hazardous" and "gold_fixed → □same_rulers" and "military_unhappy" and "□(city_happy → some_part_happy)"
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "keeping the same persons always in office becomes the cause of sedition", "the golden particle God mixed into the soul does not pass from one to another but always stays with the same", "Socrates deprives the military of happiness", "necessarily, Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "necessarily, (if the whole city is happy, then all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy)", "necessarily, (if the whole city is happy, then all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy)", "necessarily, Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office" and "necessarily, (if the whole city is happy, then all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy)" are true and "settling the magistracy as Socrates has done is very hazardous" is false. Is S1, "It is also very hazardous to settle the magistracy as Socrates has done; for he would have persons of the same rank always in office, which becomes the cause of sedition even amongst those who are of no account, but more particularly amongst those who are of a courageous and warlike disposition; it is indeed evidently necessary that he should frame his community in this manner; for that golden particle which God has mixed up in the soul of man flies not from one to the other, but always continues with the same; for he says, that some of our species have gold, and others silver, blended in their composition from the moment of their birth: but those who are to be husbandmen and artists, brass and iron; besides, though he deprives the military of happiness, he says, that the legislator ought to make all the citizens happy; but it is impossible that the whole city can be happy, without all, or the greater, or some part of it be happy.", true then?
aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 has "sedition → hazardous" and "hazardous ∧ ¬city_happy → defects"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3 have "hazardous ∧ ¬city_happy → defects"; they differ when only "keeping the same persons always in office becomes the cause of sedition" is true, which the second allows
